{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's distinction between 'risk-minimization' and 'induction' collapses because any strategy that prefers Theory A over Theory B based on past test results necessarily assumes that the conditions which made A superior in the past will remain relevant or operative in the future application context. Popper explicitly rejected the principle of the uniformity of nature; therefore, acting on corroboration without assuming some continuity between past performance and future reliability is logically incoherent. The claim that one can treat corroboration merely as a 'record of error-elimination' while using it to guide future action smuggles in the very inductive assumption (that eliminated errors are indicative of future stability) that the argument claims to avoid.","premise":"Acting on past test results to predict future suitability entails an assumption of nature's uniformity, which constitutes induction.","successor_question":"If corroboration provides no basis for expecting future success, on what non-inductive grounds can a scientist prefer a well-tested bridge design over an untested one when lives are at stake?","target":"7ed42303bed8b5a3f186af1cbae3b2735f839c8793ce159068ac03d43fa89395"}]}